## Why this phase writes no test file

`docs/context/methodology.md:30-36` names this case exactly:

> It does **not** mean writing React component tests first: the same document
> deliberately keeps UI verification manual, and that split is unchanged. A
> PRD whose acceptance criteria are purely visual produces no test file — that
> is the `EXISTING_COVERAGE_SUFFICIENT` / no-test-required path, not a
> violation.

Phase 4 is the verification phase. Its four code changes are ARIA attributes
and one accessible name — `role`/`aria-live` on the toast, the date input's
`aria-label`, `role="status"` on the deck's offline hint, `aria-describedby`
on the inline title editor. None of them adds a decision this project can
test: there is no DOM in the only test tier (Vitest inside workerd), no
component tier exists (`documentation/40-engineering/testing-strategy.md:33`
marks "React components and layout" as **No — manual**), and there is no
browser tier and no plan to buy one before unit 6
(`testing-strategy.md:37`, `documentation/50-planning/ui-ux-plan.md:113`).
Everything else the phase produces is a record: the Level A walk, the build
report, the owner's runbook.

Writing a test here would mean either asserting on a JSX string (a snapshot of
markup, which pins nothing about behaviour) or standing up a DOM the project
has deliberately not bought. Both are worse than the honest answer: the four
changes are pinned by the plan's own per-task `VALIDATE` greps and by the
Level 3 structural gate, and their effect is verified where it is observable
— in the browser pane and on the owner's devices.

